  "id": "DrugBank_DB00146 - MONDO_313",
  "verbalization": "Calcifediol, a vitamin D analog, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypophosphatemia (disease) through a mechanism involving Calcitriol. The evidence suggests that Calcifediol is associated with the development of Renal Osteodystrophy and Nephrocalcinosis, both of which are linked to Calcitriol. Calcitriol, in turn, is known to induce Hypophosphatemia, as it enhances phosphate reabsorption in the kidneys. The cumulative evidence from the three paths indicates a potential pathway where Calcifediol stimulates Calcitriol production, which then leads to impaired phosphate homeostasis and the development of Hypophosphatemia. The scores reflect the strength of the associations, with the highest confidence in the link between Calcifediol and Hypophosphatemia through the Calcitriol-mediated pathway. The mechanism is supported by the documented interactions between Calcifediol and Calcitriol, and the observed clinical implications in renal disorders.",
